Date: Mon, 18 Jan 93 18:27:51 PST
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: PPL: Legal Credit Ratings (was: Why Not Let Convicts ...)

Tom Morrow writes:
>I think, instead, that a victim would have to *pay* to earn the exclusive
>right to the information generated by the operations of a private legal
>system that catches and convicts a criminal.  Remember, legal credit is a
>form of intellectual property that others will find very useful.
>... the criminal would have to pay
>to keep his or her record clean.  But note that reputational interests of
>those who compile and sell "legal credit" ratings may preclude them from
>offering exemption at moderate prices.

Tom is right.  Innocent victims are not relevant here.  Since the
credit agency is in a sense your agent, validating your claims of
trustworthyness, the negotiation is really between you (the criminal)
and you (the rated person).  If I sign up for a law which allows
verdicts to be hidden, then people who judge my credit record will
have to take that into account, knowing that it might not show the
whole story.  If, on the other hand, you signed up for a law that
requires that your credit agency be told of convictions, then a clean
record there will mean a lot more.

If you have to give permission for someone to be able to see your
credit report, then it seems clear to me that you want to commit to
having convictions go on the record.  After all, if you wanted it,
your credit agency could issue special "edited" reports, which say
"All the things that happened on this report are true, but other
events may have been deleted at the person in question's request".
For example, it might show all judgements of more than $1000,
validating that you have never murdered, but silent on whether you've
stepped on your neighbor's flowers.

So I predict laws would typically tell about convictions to credit
agencies.  

To complicate matters, victims are often not entirely innocent, and
having been a victim several times can be a credit strike against you,
as with car accidents.  So I predict laws would typically tell both
side's credit agencies of the event, but the criminal's agency would
not have the right to know who was the victim, and the victim's
agency would not know who was the criminal.  If credit consumers
wanted to know more statistics about the other person in the crimes
you've been involved in, things might get messy.  Imagine someone
wanted to be sure there wasn't a racial bias in the folks you've
punched.

Robin Hanson

