Date: Mon, 18 Jan 93 21:15:38 CST
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: Immortalist conservatism

Robin Hanson says of an immortalist:

>You would still face the difficult choice of staying in a career
>you've been in a long time, and so are good at and getting better, vs.
>switching and doing something more fun, but at a cost in wages,
>prestige, power etc. . . .
>So I predict "immortals" will stay in careers for a *very* long time.

I think this misses the point of my post.  I never said that immortalists
will always move on to other careers, or even that they are more likely to
do so than auto-deathists (aka ephemeralists).  My argument merely claimed
that immortalists will be more willing to take risks with regard to long
term commitments because they are more free to try other long term
commitments if they later choose to.

To put it in economic terms, think of life span as capital which its holder
can invest in long term commitments.  An immortalist has more of this
capital, and can thus purchase (in serial fashion) many long term
commitments than an ephemeralist can.  The immortalist's opportunity costs
of taking on any particular long term commitment are thus lower; he does
not give up all other such commitments by choosing only one.  The
immortalist is thus more willing to take on risky long term commitments
than an ephemeralist is; he has less to lose.



