Date: Mon, 18 Jan 93 21:53:09 CST
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: PPL: Legal Credit Ratings

Pursuing the concept of legal credit, Robin Hanson writes:

>If you have to give permission for someone to be able to see your
>credit report, then it seems clear to me that you want to commit to
>having convictions go on the record.  After all, if you wanted it,
>your credit agency could issue special "edited" reports, which say
>"All the things that happened on this report are true, but other
>events may have been deleted at the person in question's request".
>For example, it might show all judgements of more than $1000,
>validating that you have never murdered, but silent on whether you've
>stepped on your neighbor's flowers.

Yes, this seems right.  It also accords with what I've been experiencing in
my attempt to get car insurance--potential insurers ask me how many moving
violations I've had in the last three years.  They do not car about parking
tickets.  And sometimes they ask whether I have *ever* had my license
suspended.  

But note that in this example the data control is being done on the
receiving end, rather than the collecting and compiling one.  By analogy,
we might expect a legal credit agency to collect all information but offer
different levels of disclosure for different prices.  Then those purchasing
the information could pay for only as much information as they can afford,
and legal credit agencies could keep their reputations entact.  Criminals
could still protect their reputations, however, by bidding against the
disclosure of their legal credit ratings.  This would result in a gambling
game whereby, for example, an employer would offer jobs at 1/2 pay with no
background check and risk that the costs of attracting convicts would not
exceed the payroll savings.

I find this area very interesting, as it brings together two of my favorite
topics:  PPL and intellectual property.  Until we began to discuss the
finer points of these overlapping topics, I had not fully realized how
powerful and interesting a role legal credit may well play in a PPL.  

I have begun to wonder if legal credit might not serve to bridge the gap
between the restitution due to victims and the level of penalty at which
monetary fines for crimes would have to be set to effectively deter
profit-seeking criminals.  As has often been noted, the fact that only a
fraction of all criminals gets caught can render criminal activity
profitable for repeat offenders who pay only restitution for their crimes. 
This may be true even if restitution includes costs of apprehension and
trial.  Including insurance costs would bridge the gap, but I believe there
are moral grounds to object to forcing a caught criminal to effectively pay
for the risks imposed by other, unapprehended criminals.

Now I'm beginning to wonder if the ostracism (partial or complete) allowed
by legal credit might not raise the caught criminal's costs enough to
bridge the gap between the profits of crime and the costs of restitution. 
So far it's just an inkling, but I suspect that there may be deep relations
between social cost, ostracism, and restitution that ensure that in a PPL
at equilibrium crime really *doesn't* pay.  I'll turn on my ponder machine
and get back to you when its done churning.


