Date: Mon, 18 Jan 93 23:22:51 CST
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: PPL: Legal Credit Ratings

Marc Ringuette posts, with regard to legal credit:

>I don't think it's going to be practically
>possible for a criminal to pay extra to suppress publication of a
>crime.  

Well, at least not at collection point.  As I have noted, legal credit
agencies will have strong incentives to protect their reputations as
collectors of complete and accurate criminal records.  Would you buy a
legal credit rating from a company known to let criminals pay to have their
records purged?  Great--then you'll only miss the *really effective*
criminals!  

But I think criminals may be able to buy off the disclosure of their legal
credit ratings at the other end of the data stream, where the credit
ratings would otherwise go to an interested third party purchaser.  As I
noted in an earlier post, the criminal need only offer the purchaser
something having a value at least equal to what the putative purchaser
perceives her costs of dealing with a mysterious stranger would be.

>. . . agencies could ferret out informants by carefully
>controlling who knows about a given incident, and matching employees with
>known leaks.

Yes, the records of criminal activity gathered and recorded by private law
enforcement agencies should qualify as trade secrets.  This is one sort of
intellectual property that libertarians can embrace without embarassment,
as it grows out of contract and tort law.  An employee of an agency that
deals in legal credit ratings would be bound by either an express or
implied contract to preserve the secrecy of his employer's records.

As an aside, I note that those who purchase legal credit ratings are
actually buying more than just information--they're buying a service that
ensures that its legal credit ratings are up to rate.  The same service
would probably also process raw criminal records to come up with
easy-to-digest risk ratings.  So when you think of what a legal credit
agency would sell, don't think of a long list of names and crimes.  Think
of the teleservice that merchants use when they check the balance on your
credit card.





